mind your decisions I'm a presto Walker  in a semi-circle draw a chord with a  length of six draw another chord with a  length of six and a third chord with a  length of 14 the question is to solve  for the radius of the semicircle I  adapted this problem from a tweet by  Bilal Saddam Ashley pause if you'd like  to give this problem a try and when  you're ready keep watching to learn how  to solve this problem  [Music]  we'll get started by drawing a cord to  connect the two cords that have a length  of six because these two cords have  equal lengths the angles opposite them  will have equal measure  therefore the arcs that correspond to  these inscribed angles will also have  the same measure  now draw this cord of the semicircle the  inscribed angles will correspond to  equal arcs and therefore they will have  equal measure equal to theta next the  semicircle will have a diameter of two  times its radius an inscribed angle in a  semicircle will have a ninety degree  measure and be a right angle therefore  sine of theta will be equal to 6 over 2r  we'll draw another chord this is also an  inscribed angle of the semicircle  therefore it's a right angle  consequently cosine of two theta will be  equal to 14 over 2r we can now focus on  these two equations cosine of two theta  will be equal to one minus two times the  sine squared of theta and we can get  sine squared of theta from the second  equation we also multiply the second  equation by two and then we can  substitute into the first equation we  now multiply both sides by two R squared  and then simplify this quadratic  equation since the radius has to be  greater than zero we get the solution  that R is equal to nine and that's the  answer to this problem I love how this  seemingly simple geometry problem leads  to a quadratic equation and a fittingly  nice answer thanks for making mind your  decisions one of the best channels on  YouTube as always thanks for watching  and thanks for your support  [Music]
 

